overdoses relapses and Rehab doesn't  sound like a recipe for a successful  comedy But Chuck Laurie pulled it off  for eight seasons with his Smash Hit mom  if you've ever dreamed of making it in  Showbiz and need a bit of inspiration  check out Chuck Laurie's story Laurie  the creator of mom along with a host of  other hit sitcoms spent many years broke  and struggling before finally breaking  through in his mid-30s as he told the  Lansing State Journal I remember vividly  what it's like to put 38 cents in the  gas tank and drive to my second cousin's  house so they would feed me before  hitting it big in television however  Lori spent some time dabbling in another  creative profession songwriting and if  you're wondering whether any of his  songs became well known we've got four  words for you Teenage Mutant Ninja  Turtles yes if you've ever gotten the  Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles theme song  stuck in your head you can thank Lori  who co-wrote the catchy song that helped  Define the franchise as for his comedy  writing dream that didn't really happen  until he got a job writing for the  groundbreaking sitcom Roseanne a major  break that opened doors launching him on  a career trajectory like no other after  Roseanne Laurie went on to become one of  the most successful sitcom creators in  history responsible for the hits Grace  Under fire Sybil and Dharma and Greg but  Lori really hit the jackpot with Two and  a Half Men And The Big Bang Theory the  pair of Mega hits which gave him the  Hollywood cred to make Mom happen and  even though Laurie is a rarity a  sitcom's fangali with multiple Mega hits  under his belt any aspiring Rider can  find inspiration in his rags to Rich his  tail  before Mom Anna Faris was best known for  her roles in comedies like just friends  The House Bunny and the scary movie  franchise with her distinctive comedic  chops and bubbly blonde Persona she was  the first actor CBS approached for Mom  Ferris told pop sugar that her husband  at the time actor Chris Pratt actually  read the script for Mom before she did  and by the time he got to the second  page he was convinced the role was  perfect for her saying honey whatever  you're doing stop now and read this even  though television has come a long way  since the days where everything edgy was  on HBO Ferris and Janie agreed that Mom  dealt with risky issues for Network  sitcom during a joint interview with  Larry King when King asked Ferris if she  was ever surprised by the content of the  show she revealed that she was  constantly shocked by how far the  writers were willing to push it saying  on a weekly basis when we get the new  scripts I always say are we sure CBS is  okay with this  before Mom Allison Janney was best known  for her role as White House Press  Secretary Claudia Jean Craig also known  as CJ on The West Wing fans may be  surprised to learn that she didn't have  her breakthrough role on The West Wing  until she was 40 which is pretty unusual  by Hollywood standards Janie did have a  steady stream of supporting movie and TV  roles before then but nothing as big as  the West Wing which eventually led to  Mom  on Mom Allison janny's character Bonnie  had long flowing auburn hair her quaff  actually became synonymous with the  character which was a bit ironic  considering that it's not even Janie's  real hair yep the actor was wearing a  wig the whole time and her co-workers  didn't even know Jenny's real hair is  actually a beautiful shade of natural  silver and when she showed up on set  without the wig one day The Producers  apparently freaked during an appearance  on The Tonight Show starring Jimmy  Fallon Jenny told the tale how are we  gonna do what you should have asked us  before you did this to your hair and I'm  like guys I've been wearing a wig since  season one but no one nobody knew it as  a recovering addict himself mom was a  very personal show for Chuck Laurie who  got sober himself at the age of 47 as he  told TV Guide sobriety is a big part of  my life and it's been that way for  almost 13 years I'm eternally grateful  I've been able to find this in my life  I'm trying so hard I quit drinking I'm  going to meetings as far as how his  personal recovery Journey relates to Mom  Laura I told indiewire broken people  trying to repair their lives I can  relate to that I think we're all in the  process of doing a little mending  excuse me no one's going to be snorting  any fat rails while I'm here and as  Janie told Indie wire in another  interview it feels like a very grown-up  show for Chuck Laurie I had no idea that  we were going to be going to the places  that we've gotten to go with Mom  in Show Business like any other business  success breeds success and in Chuck  Lori's case having so many hits on his  resume gave him the freedom to take on  riskier stuff in other words shows like  two and a half men gave him the freedom  to make Mom Laurie told indiewire that  to challenge himself creatively he  needed to tackle something different  something that would force him to step  into new areas of Comedy I am at a point  in my career where I can take some  chances if you're going to experiment do  something that is Meaningful to you if  you fail you did something meaningful to  you and with Mom he did just that  someone's spoiling themselves actually  it's for my mom  she's a drug addict who just got out of  jail  oh  back in the 1970s legendary TV producer  Norman Lear was a household name he had  many Creations to his name but none is  groundbreaking as All in the Family  which dealt with topical issues and  proved that TV sitcoms could be  intelligent and thought-provoking Chuck  Glory often credits Lear for inspiring  him to create similarly edgy sitcoms  proving to the world that television  doesn't have to be predictable and it  doesn't need to be insulting to the  intelligence of the audience in an  interview with variety Laurie said that  with Mom he wanted to tell stories that  could take place in what he calls a  Norman Lear World in other words realism  rather than fantasy you know the  definition of insanity yes I know the  definition of insanity I live with you  Lear now 100 years old and someone Lori  considers A friend brought a reality to  the sitcom format that had never been  seen before and much like Lori has done  with Mom Lear made history tackling  tough subjects on TV as well in an  interview with the Los Angeles Times  Lear noted people will tell you you  can't do just about anything mention  abortion have a gay character write  funny about cancer you do it anyway  Christy Plunkett Jill's sponsor  but I'm not responsible for this  it was a big surprise to the viewers of  mom that the character of Jody played by  Emily Osmond had a fatal overdose and  died on the show at that point Jody had  reached six months of sobriety Lori has  said it was decided that Jody would have  an overdose when she was first  introduced to the show he explained the  reasoning behind this in an interview  with TV Insider recovery from addiction  is fragile the one day at a time Trope  is sadly true it's a vulnerable state of  being that requires vigilance you can't  ignore it the entire cast of mom was  heartbroken when they read the script  and realized that Jody was going to die  on the show not only did they love the  character but they loved Osment as well  it was a challenge for the writers of  mom to figure out how to bring tragedy  to the show and then go back to being a  comedy But ultimately Jody's death  showed that life goes on  laughter is contagious and the shared  experience of laughing can make a comedy  even funnier this is why it was so hard  to shoot mom when the covid-19 pandemic  took away its live audience  something I said changed the course of  your life for the better  it feels like it are you listening to  this veteran TV director James widows  who held episodes of mom agreed with  that assessment speaking to variety he  called shooting the sitcom during the  pandemic a logistical challenge like no  other Gemma Baker the co-creator of mom  told deadline that not having a live  audience was one of the hardest parts of  finally ending the series an audience  was so much a part of what we did and  this whole season we missed them it  really was a huge loss to not have that  energy and feedback and laughter it  would have been great if we could have  ended the season in front of a live  audience  when Anna Faris announced that she was  leaving Mom after seven seasons it was a  big shock to say the least and while  it's hard for a show to survive when a  lead character leaves it isn't unheard  of similar to Chuck Laurie's other hit  sitcom Two and a Half Men mom was able  to hang in there for a little while  longer putting out one more season  however co-creator Gemma Baker wishes  they could have done more with Christy  telling deadline there were so many  things that we wanted to address that we  didn't get a chance to if we had a  little more time we would have gotten  the chance one storyline fans missed out  on was seeing how Christy would have  done at Georgetown University law school  Baker described how she imagined it  Christy would have faced some challenges  But ultimately would have overcome them  Baker went on to tell deadline how she  envisioned closure for Christy I like to  think that Christy got into a happy and  healthy relationship and that she is  still a part of her mother's life  fans can have a lot of power when it  comes to saving a show case in point the  original Star Trek series about to be  canceled after the second season NBC's  Deluge of letters from fans begging the  network to keep it on saved it the cop  drama Cagney and Lacey has a similar  story after it was canceled a letter  writing campaign kept it on the air and  when Mom was officially canceled in  February 2021 fans mobilized with a 50  000 signature petition and those fans  who were in recovery were particularly  passionate to them it was important for  the show to remain on the air because of  the fact that so many addicts were  struggling during the pandemic as the  petition stated mom has been a crucial  voice in destigmatizing addiction this  show has no doubt saved countless lives  by promoting substance abuse recovery  and encouraging people to seek help  while the show did end up going off the  air the spirit of mom and how it gave a  voice to people in recovery will carry  on  a lot of sitcoms have endings where  everything ends up just fine and  everyone lives happily ever after but  for a show like Mom having everything  wrapped up with a neat little bow at the  end just wouldn't work  my name is Bonnie  and I'm a grateful alcoholic  co-creator Gemma Baker also didn't want  a convenient ending for the show telling  The Hollywood Reporter because of the  nature of the show it didn't feel  truthful to have an ending where  everything is tied up because the show  has always really been about reality and  characters showing up for Life On Life's  terms I think it's true what we say here  don't leave before the miracle  oh  Baker recalled how the finale was hard  to put together because they wanted to  bring the show to a close in a way that  was meaningful and that did the best for  the characters and the audience as well  a challenging feat even under the best  of circumstances made more difficult by  the sudden departure of the lead we'll  be there every step of the way  stay straight  and the characters and the creative  staff had to make the best of a tough  situation and deal with life on life's  terms forgiven  [Music]
 

